WEBVTT

cue-VID_U2_02-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Ironbridge Freight Depot has five management layers. One supervisor directs three loaders; a route change takes six days. A tall structure has many levels and a narrow span of control. A flat structure has fewer levels and a wider span. The chain of command shows who reports to whom. Watch the chart and the waiting loaders.

cue-VID_U2_02-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The structure morphs. Two middle layers slide away and the chart collapses from five levels to three. The span of control widens from three loaders per supervisor to eight. Lines redraw in blue. Instructions pass from director to supervisor to loader. The chain of command is shorter, but each supervisor must coach eight people instead of three. Delayering changes both speed and workload.

cue-VID_U2_02-3
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Span of control is the number of subordinates working directly under a manager. Arrows fan to eight loaders. A narrow span gives close support but adds cost and slows decisions. A wide span saves cost but leaves less time per person. At Ironbridge Freight Depot, eight loaders is the largest span that still allows a daily safety check.

cue-VID_U2_02-4
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Managers plan, organise, coordinate, command and control. Delegation means giving a subordinate the authority to perform particular tasks. The depot manager hands the shift leader authority to approve overtime up to five hundred dollars. Authority moves down the arrow; responsibility stays with the manager. Delegation develops staff and speeds decisions, but it can cause inconsistency if two shift leaders set different limits. Clear guidelines reduce that risk.

cue-VID_U2_02-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Downsizing is permanently reducing the number of people a business employs. Ironbridge removes eight posts, including two coordination roles, as automation takes over paperwork. Staff fall from forty to thirty-two. These workers are made redundant, not dismissed. Redundancy arises when a job is no longer needed; dismissal concerns conduct or capability. Compensation and retraining help the remaining team cover the wider span.

cue-VID_U2_02-6
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Three decision flows run side by side. Autocratic leaders decide alone and have orders followed without consultation. A closure order reaches the loader in seconds. Democratic leaders consult, then decide themselves. The team debates, then the manager chooses. Laissez-faire leaders set broad objectives and leave capable staff to organise their own work. The style must fit urgency, risk and skill.

cue-VID_U2_02-7
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:58.000
A trade union is a group of employees who have joined together to ensure their interests and working conditions are protected. Representatives meet the manager monthly. Collective bargaining lifts the hourly rate from twelve dollars to twelve dollars and sixty cents. Members gain a voice, better pay and support in disputes. The depot gains clearer communication, though wage costs rise and industrial action can stop deliveries. Both sides must prepare evidence.

cue-VID_U2_02-8
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Consider the trade-offs on screen. A tall structure gives clear promotion paths and close supervision, but decisions crawl through five layers. A flat structure cuts cost and speeds decisions, yet supervisors are stretched and promotion paths narrow. Autocratic leadership is fast in a crisis, but can damage morale. Democratic leadership wins commitment, but takes time. Laissez-faire suits skilled professionals, but fails with inexperienced staff. There is no single best structure: match it to the situation and the evidence.

cue-VID_U2_02-9
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The Ironbridge story in numbers. Because five layers slowed every approval, therefore delayering removed two layers, so a route change now takes one day instead of six. Headcount fell from forty to thirty-two through redundancy. Supervisors now coach eight loaders each. Daily consignments rose from one hundred and twenty to one hundred and thirty-four. Union bargaining set the rate at twelve dollars and sixty cents. Weigh the saving against the wider workload. One decision creates both a benefit and a cost.

